WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.960
Even without the iconic prologue crawl or
that classic "A long time ago..." text that

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:08.570
precedes most entries in the saga, most people
could be dragged into a theater showing a

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:13.040
Star Wars film they've never seen and still
know exactly what they were watching within

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:13.540
moments.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:14.400
Here's why.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:19.339
Something really, really bad always happens
pretty much every time a bottomless shaft

00:00:19.339 --> 00:00:20.720
appears in a Star Wars movie.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.919
If the saga's heroes had any sense at all,
they would've learned by now that whenever

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:27.960
they walk into a room with a bottomless shaft,
they need to turn around and run the hell

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:28.460
away.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:32.160
Qui-Gon meets his violent end at the foot
of one of these impossibly long tunnels in

00:00:32.169 --> 00:00:33.300
Phantom Menace.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:37.340
Both halves of Darth Maul plummet down one
of those shafts, just like Darth Vader hurls

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:39.770
the Emperor down one in Return of the Jedi.

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:44.020
Luke miraculously survives a drop down Cloud
City's bottomless shaft in The Empire Strikes

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:48.560
Back, and Obi-Wan hops over one to turn off
the Death Star's tractor beam in A New Hope.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:52.540
The heroes of Rogue One also climb up one
to get hold of the Death Star plans.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.260
And then there's The Force Awakens frankly,
Han should have known better than to walk

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:57.960
out over one.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:02.060
The shafts of Star Wars are legion, and if
you see one, you're probably in big trouble.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:08.420
Star Wars movies tend to be bad for limbs
and probably very kind to black market prosthetics

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:08.920
dealers.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:12.420
Someone loses a limb in most entries, and,
whether you're watching the movies based on

00:01:12.429 --> 00:01:17.170
theatrical release or in chronological order,
it's not going to take long before someone

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:19.240
loses something very important to them.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:24.540
Darth Maul loses everything below his waist
in Phantom Menace and is revealed to have

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:29.079
survived the ordeal in the Star Wars: The
Clone Wars animated series, even making a

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.400
cameo in Solo: A Star Wars Story.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:36.320
Anakin Skywalker says goodbye to the first
of many soon-to-be-lost limbs when Count Dooku

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:41.729
relieves him of an arm in Attack of the Clones,
and he loses the remaining arm and both legs

00:01:41.729 --> 00:01:43.000
in Revenge of the Sith.

00:01:43.209 --> 00:01:47.760
No major characters lose limbs in A New Hope,
though Obi-Wan does leave a cantina bully's

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:53.520
bloody arm on the bar and later loses his
entire body, if you wanna count that.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:58.110
Luke takes off a Wampa's hairy arm in Empire
Strikes Back, his father sends Luke's hand

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:02.249
spinning down a shaft later in the film, and
Luke pays him back by taking off one of Vader's

00:02:02.249 --> 00:02:04.539
already replaced hands in Return of the Jedi.

00:02:04.539 --> 00:02:08.920
Neither of the newest trilogy's released entries
feature limb loss, but both had deleted scenes

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:09.920
with amputations.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.920
Chewbacca tears off Unkar Plutt's arm in a
snipped Force Awakens scene, and The Last

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:19.180
Jedi originally had Captain Phasma's hand
sliced off by Finn just before her death.

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:24.440
First introduced in Empire Strikes Back, Yoda
has proven to be one of the most popular and

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:26.760
oft-quoted characters in the Star Wars mythos.

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:31.600
From his first scenes in Empire, the Jedi
Master earned a reputation as a near-bottomless

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:32.760
font of wisdom.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:34.060
"Great warrior?

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:39.880
Wars not make one great, hm?"

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.860
Although, with the advent of CGI and the release
of Attack of the Clones, the syntax-challenged

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:49.140
Jedi was able to prove himself a badass warrior
as well.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:54.070
But Yoda's also part of a Star Wars tradition
that doesn't begin or end on Dagobah.

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:58.040
Almost every entry in the franchise includes
at least one character like Yoda someone who

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:02.690
initially seems physically unimpressive, but
ultimately proves to not only be an expert

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:07.590
warrior but a source of great wisdom and usually
information about the mysterious Force even

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:09.660
if the character isn't a Jedi.

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:14.740
A New Hope gave us Obi-Wan, the oldest hero
in the film's ragtag group who nevertheless

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:19.520
easily saves Luke from Ponda Baba and later
fights Darth Vader in a lightsaber duel.

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:23.900
Yoda fills the role in both Empire and Return
of the Jedi, as well as appearing in all of

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:24.880
the prequel films.

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:29.780
Chirrut Imwe is arguably the most formidable
hand-to-hand combatant to appear in Rogue

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:32.880
One as well as being the most vocal supporter
of the Force in the film.

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:36.860
The enigmatic Maz Kanata plays the part in
the newest trilogy, even though her connection

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:39.760
to the Jedi, if there is any, hasn't yet been
revealed.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.660
People have a lot of fun flying in Star Wars.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.960
That's not to say they shouldn't have fun,
especially since they're usually just a few

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:50.180
minutes away from having their arms chopped
off and thrown down a bottomless shaft.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:51.540
Why not make the most of it?

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:55.220
But it's still kinda remarkable just how much
fun they're having, and there's an almost

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:59.860
endless stream of "wahoos" and "yee-has" to
be heard throughout the saga's many stories.

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:02.300
And they usually happen in the cockpit of
a spaceship.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:06.980
Whether it's Han celebrating finally taking
out a TIE fighter or even the young Anakin

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:11.420
Skywalker belting out the occasional "wahoo"
while threading through Trade Federation droid

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:15.520
fighters, Star Wars heroes really like to
yell even when they're having a really bad

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:16.020
day.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:20.560
Take Finn, who, when he first escapes the
clutches of the First Order with Poe, is wahooing

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:25.120
up and down the dark void of space as he and
his new buddy both narrowly escape death.

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:30.500
While the Star Wars galaxy boasts a wide variety
of planets, the planets themselves don't tend

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:32.440
to offer a wide variety of climates and terrains.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:36.560
In fact, the worlds of Star Wars tend to be
planets of extremes.

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:40.970
While our own Earth has a few deserts, for
example, if there's a desert on a planet in

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:45.850
a Star Wars film then it's likely because
the entire planet is a desert like the recurring

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:49.000
setting of Tatooine or Force Awakens' Jakku.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:53.620
If there's ice and snow on the planet, there's
ice and snow everywhere, like the frigid planet

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:55.480
Hoth in the beginning of Empire Strikes Back.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:00.340
There's the swamp planet Dagobah, the ocean
planet Kamino, and the volcano planet Mustafar

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:01.400
in Revenge of the Sith.

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:07.060
The final location in Return of the Jedi is
even referred to as the "Forest Moon of Endor,"

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:10.790
in case visitors aren't quite sure of what
extreme and singular environment they should

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:11.280
expect.

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:14.200
Speaking of extreme planets, how about this?

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.660
No matter what the story is about or who the
lead character is, the Star Wars saga just

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:21.200
keeps on bringing us back to one of two desert
planets.

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:26.280
Usually it's Tatooine, though Tatooine is
replaced by the equally desolate and arid

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:28.120
Jakku in The Force Awakens.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:32.700
Even The Rise of Skywalker is poised to add
to Star Wars' roster of desert planets, with

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:34.780
the strange, rocky world of Pasaana.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:39.240
Now, it is a little surprising that Tatooine
in particular features quite so much in the

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:43.919
Star Wars mythos, if for no other reason than
a series set in a galaxy chock full of planets

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:48.240
inhabited by intelligent life keeps swinging
back to the same dry ball of sand.

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:52.620
"Well, if there's a bright center to the universe
you’re on the planet that it’s farthest

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:53.120
from."

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:58.520
While the question of Tattoine's specific
connection to the Force has yet to be directly

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:02.580
addressed in the films, it would be surprising
if its recurring appearances had nothing to

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:04.460
do with a strong connection to the Force.

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:08.960
From Luke's training on Dagobah in Empire
Strikes Back and Rey's time spent on Ahch-To

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:13.270
[AHK-toe] in The Last Jedi, we know certain
places in the universe are particularly strong

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:14.100
with the Force.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:19.020
Considering Tatooine is the birthplace of
Anakin Skywalker, the childhood home of Luke

00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:23.230
Skywalker, and the decades-long hiding place
of Obi-Wan Kenobi, it's probably fair to say

00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:27.640
that the place has at least some kind of significant
connection to the Force as a whole.

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:32.120
The universe is a big place and the Star Wars
galaxy hides many titans.

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:37.140
Star Wars boasts enough giant monsters to
fill the cast of any decent Godzilla flick.

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:41.680
Unlike most of the monsters in Godzilla, though,
Star Wars titans are weirdly good at hiding;

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:43.580
especially considering their size.

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:47.380
Often, these monsters even appear as naturally
occurring parts of the terrain.

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:51.040
Most famously, there's the Mighty Sarlacc
of Return of the Jedi who swallows

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:54.020
Boba Fett and dozens of Jabba the Hutt's henchmen.

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:58.199
From afar, the Sarlacc looks like nothing
more than a huge hole in the ground, but once

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:02.260
you get close enough to spot the teeth and
the tendrils, you're probably already lunch.

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:06.960
Equally sneaky is the gargantuan space slug
of Empire Strikes Back, who almost makes a

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:11.160
meal of the heroes when Han mistakes the beast's
insides for a cave.

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:16.280
Then there's the dianoga the beast who almost
drags Luke to his death in the trash compactor

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:18.800
scene of A New Hope — and who mostly appears
off-screen.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:23.680
The Rancor, though not exactly as cleverly
hidden as the Sarlacc or the space slug, is

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:26.350
kept out of sight for its first few scenes
in Return of the Jedi.

00:07:26.350 --> 00:07:30.560
There are also the multiple aquatic giants
who threaten the heroes of Phantom Menace

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:32.580
as they navigate Naboo's waterways.

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:38.000
And then, of course, there's the massive,
tentacled Summa-verminoth, who tries to devour

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:39.920
the Millennium Falcon in Solo.

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:42.840
Droids don't do what droids are supposed to
do.

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:46.720
Or, more commonly, they do what they're supposed
to do but only after complaining about it

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:47.960
for a really long time.

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:53.740
C-3P0 is constantly arguing against the heroes'
plans, and R2-D2 is usually preoccupied with

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:54.880
some secret mission.

00:07:54.920 --> 00:08:00.580
In Rogue One, K-2 second-guesses all of Cassian's
decisions and sets himself up as the authority

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:05.220
on who can and who cannot be trusted, while
in Solo, L3 is more concerned with starting

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:07.380
a droid revolution than with being helpful.

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:11.960
Ironically, perhaps the best evidence of this
is in the rare example of a droid doing what

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:12.800
it's supposed to do.

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:17.400
In Phantom Menace, R2-D2 is introduced as
one of a number of droids who are dispatched

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:20.260
to repair Queen Amidala's ship
while under fire.

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:24.780
R2 is the only droid to survive and manages
to fix the ship's hyperdrive.

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:29.620
Later, Amidala thanks the droid for its service
and orders it be specially cleaned as a reward.

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:33.940
Which means, apparently, a droid doing precisely
what it was designed to do is such a rare

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:37.770
occurrence in the Star Wars narrative that
when it actually happens, any nearby humans

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:40.380
feel the need to set up a freaking awards
ceremony.

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:44.840
The Mos Eisley Cantina from A New Hope is
the setting of one of the franchise's most

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:47.880
well-remembered scenes and also one of its
most replicated.

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:53.020
Similar hives "of scum and villainy" can be
found in just about every Star Wars film.

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:57.500
Jabba's palace in Return of the Jedi is basically
a larger, grander version of the Mos Eisley

00:08:57.500 --> 00:08:58.100
Cantina.

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:02.760
In one of Attack of the Clones' few genuinely
funny moments, Obi-Wan uses his Jedi mind

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:06.570
trick to convince a drug dealer in a bar on
Coruscant to rethink his path

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:07.060
in life.

00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:11.680
Fittingly, Solo features almost nothing but
these places of ill repute, starting with

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:16.800
the crime boss lair Han escapes on Corellia,
the casino on Vandor where Han first meets

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:21.130
Lando, and later the bar on Numidian Prime
where Han finally wins the Millenium Falcon

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:21.900
from Lando.

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:26.940
Shortly after Han meets Finn and Rey in The
Force Awakens, he brings them to Maz Kanata's

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:28.930
cantina-esque castle on Takodana.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:34.040
And while its leader and soldiers were extremist
rebels rather than bounty hunters and criminals,

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:37.620
Saw Gerrera's stronghold on Jedha felt a lot
like Jabba's Palace.

00:09:37.780 --> 00:09:42.660
Then there are the examples that seem more
"upscale," but are nevertheless just as villainous

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:47.570
like Dryden Vos' yacht in Solo and the casino
that caters to the super rich residents of

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:49.460
Canto Bight in The Last Jedi.

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:52.040
They're fancy, sure, but that doesn't make
them any nicer.

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:56.520
Obviously, if you're going to have a bunch
of hives of scum and villainy in your galaxy-spanning

00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:00.410
epic, you're going to need some actual scum
and villains populate them.

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:04.990
Luckily, the Star Wars mythos has no shortage
of smugglers, bounty hunters, organized crime

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:07.220
bosses, and other assortments of crooks and
criminals.

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:11.520
Han Solo pretends to be the very picture of
apathy in the face of the Empire's many crimes,

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:14.280
but eventually his heroism does shine through.

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:18.900
Lando joins him in Empire Strikes Back, and
Solo introduces us to even more rogues like

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:20.060
Beckett and Val.

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:22.920
Cassian of Rogue One is a different kind of
scoundrel, though.

00:10:23.020 --> 00:10:27.040
While Solo acts as if he only cares about
himself but nevertheless always winds up doing

00:10:27.050 --> 00:10:31.730
the right thing, Cassian tends to do the wrong
thing for completely unselfish reasons like

00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:35.170
when he murders another rebel operative in
the beginning of Rogue One to stop him from

00:10:35.170 --> 00:10:37.510
being captured and revealing rebel secrets.

00:10:37.510 --> 00:10:41.440
Or when he becomes more than willing to murder
Galen Erso for the sake of the rebellion.

00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:46.820
One of the most recent additions to Star Wars'
list of famous scoundrels is DJ in The Last

00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:47.320
Jedi.

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:51.920
With DJ, the storytellers turned the usual
figure of the Star Wars scoundrel on its head.

00:10:51.930 --> 00:10:56.770
The audience is led to believe that DJ is
another reluctant hero simply feigning selfishness,

00:10:56.770 --> 00:11:01.270
but he turns out to be exactly what he seems
betraying Finn and Rose to the mercies of

00:11:01.270 --> 00:11:01.960
the First Order.

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:07.080
One of the funniest ironies of the Star Wars
saga is just how many of its major characters

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:09.060
have no idea who their parents are.

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:13.860
In our comparatively technologically primitive
real world, just about anyone in the United

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:19.029
States could mail a DNA sample off and potentially
find out what part of the world their ancestors

00:11:19.029 --> 00:11:19.740
hailed from.

00:11:20.180 --> 00:11:24.850
But in a galaxy far, far away, technology
seems to be far superior to our own and no

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:27.200
one seems to have heard of DNA testing.

00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:31.380
While speculation about Rey's parents is still
running wild, she's hardly the first character

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:33.780
in the saga to face a mystery surrounding
her birth.

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:38.330
Luke Skywalker learns Darth Vader is his father
at the end of Empire Strikes Back, and Leia

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:43.330
who, unlike Luke, has met who she believes
to be her biological father and has less reason

00:11:43.330 --> 00:11:47.200
to believe there was anything mysterious at
all about her birth learns she's Luke's sister

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:48.490
in Return of the Jedi.

00:11:48.490 --> 00:11:50.180
Then, of course, there's Vader himself.

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:55.560
In Phantom Menace, Anakin's mother Shmi tells
Qui-Gon her son has no biological father.

00:11:55.560 --> 00:12:00.459
One day she wasn't pregnant, and the next
day she was, without any inciting event.

00:12:00.460 --> 00:12:02.400
And that's about as mysterious as it gets.

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:07.880
A Star Wars movie with no lightsaber fighting
is surely no Star Wars movie at all.

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:13.460
"An elegant weapon from a more civilized age."

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:18.780
In the earliest films, the duel usually takes
place toward the end.

00:12:19.160 --> 00:12:23.660
Obi-Wan battles Vader in A New Hope, Luke's
Empire Strikes Back fight with Vader is perhaps

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:27.800
the most iconic of all the duels, and, once
he's fully trained, Luke proves his skills

00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:29.860
superior to Vader's in Return of the Jedi.

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:35.950
The prequels brought us the manically choreographed
battle between Darth Maul, Qui-Gon, and Obi-Wan

00:12:35.950 --> 00:12:36.740
in Phantom Menace.

00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:40.980
Obi-Wan and Anakin are both humbled by Count
Dooku in Attack of the Clones, though they're

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:45.280
saved when Yoda busts out his crazy lightsaber
skills for the first time on-screen.

00:12:45.580 --> 00:12:49.580
Revenge of the Sith was the first film to
deliver a duel right at the beginning a rematch

00:12:49.590 --> 00:12:54.510
between Anakin and Dooku along with the Jedi
Council's doomed attempt to capture Palpatine,

00:12:54.510 --> 00:12:59.029
General Grievous' four-armed assault on Obi-Wan,
Yoda's fight with Palpatine, and Kenobi's

00:12:59.029 --> 00:13:00.980
victory over Anakin on Mustafar.

00:13:01.660 --> 00:13:05.390
The Force Awakens was the first time the series
portrayed a woman on either side of a lightsaber

00:13:05.390 --> 00:13:09.660
duel, as Rey takes on Kylo Ren, after the
latter easily defeats Finn.

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:13.220
Weirdly, there's little saber-on-saber action
in The Last Jedi.

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:18.260
Kylo and Rey fight back-to-back against Snoke's
Elite Praetorian Guard, Finn uses an energy

00:13:18.279 --> 00:13:22.610
baton rather than a lightsaber when he attacks
Phasma, and the Luke who faces Kylo at the

00:13:22.610 --> 00:13:25.720
end of the film turns out to not have been
there in the first place.

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:30.279
The only instance in which one lightsaber
physically clashes with another actually takes

00:13:30.280 --> 00:13:31.540
place during a flashback.

00:13:32.220 --> 00:13:37.320
Star Wars climaxes tend to follow one particular
multi-layered formula, in the sense that there's

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:40.500
usually one main conflict made up of two or
three smaller battles.

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:45.100
Usually, there's at least one large-scale
military clash, contrasted against a much

00:13:45.100 --> 00:13:46.660
more personal one-on-one fight.

00:13:46.900 --> 00:13:52.120
Return of the Jedi establishes the formula:
on Endor, the Ewoks and the rebel ground forces

00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:56.000
fight the Imperial troops to take out the
Death Star's shield generators, and in space

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:59.899
the rebel fleet clashes with the Imperial
TIE fighters and Star Destroyers.

00:13:59.899 --> 00:14:04.120
Meanwhile, on the Death Star, Luke and Vader
cross lightsabers one last time.

00:14:04.120 --> 00:14:06.080
The formula returns in Phantom Menace.

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:10.950
The Gungan army faces off against the droids
on Naboo, Obi-Wan and Qui-Gon have their fateful

00:14:10.950 --> 00:14:14.800
duel with Darth Maul in the Palace, while
Naboo fighters battle the Trade Federation's

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:16.000
droid ships in space.

00:14:16.209 --> 00:14:20.460
Likewise, the end of The Force Awakens is
split between the rebel fleet's attempt to

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:23.540
take out Starkiller Base and the lightsaber
duel in the snow.

00:14:23.620 --> 00:14:28.680
Rogue One's tragic final clash depicts yet
another space battle, a large-scale ground

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:34.240
assault, and the efforts of Jyn, Cassian,
and K2SO to find and transmit the Death Star

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:34.740
plans.

00:14:34.940 --> 00:14:40.180
Revenge of the Sith is one of the few entries
to not include a huge military battle — instead

00:14:40.190 --> 00:14:44.589
going back and forth between the Obi-Wan and
Vader duel on Mustafar, and the fight between

00:14:44.589 --> 00:14:46.320
Yoda and Palpatine on Coruscant.

00:14:46.980 --> 00:14:51.960
A New Hope's all-out assault on the Death
Star actually seems pretty simple in comparison.

